    "summary": "Pakistan's Defense Minister Khawaja Asif said Houthi attacks on Saudi Arabia could activate the Makkah Defense Alliance, a mutual defense pact between Pakistan, Saudi Arabia, and Turkey. The warning follows recent Houthi strikes on Saudi cities and oil facilities.",
    "content": "Pakistan's Defense Minister Khawaja Asif issued a warning on Wednesday that ongoing Houthi attacks on Saudi Arabia could trigger the Makkah Defense Alliance, a mutual defense agreement involving Pakistan, Saudi Arabia, and Turkey. Asif stated that under the pact, an attack on any member state is considered an attack on all members, potentially drawing the three nations into a coordinated response. The statement came after Houthi forces launched a series of attacks on Saudi cities and oil infrastructure in recent days.\n\nThe Makkah Defense Alliance, established in 2015, is a military coalition led by Saudi Arabia that includes several Muslim-majority countries, including Pakistan and Turkey. While the alliance was initially formed to combat Houthi rebels in Yemen, its mutual defense clause has rarely been invoked. Asif emphasized that the alliance is defensive in nature and intended to protect member states from external aggression. He did not specify what actions Pakistan might take if the pact is activated, but his remarks signal a potential escalation in regional tensions.\n\nThe Houthi movement, which controls large parts of Yemen, has stepped up attacks on Saudi territory in recent months, targeting airports, oil facilities, and military installations. Saudi Arabia has intercepted many of these attacks, but some have caused damage and casualties. The Houthis have not commented on Asif's warning, but their ongoing campaign appears aimed at pressuring Saudi Arabia to end its military intervention in Yemen, which began in 2015.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the developments are significant because many Somalis reside in Saudi Arabia and Pakistan, and any escalation could affect their security and livelihoods. Saudi Arabia hosts a large Somali expatriate community, while Pakistan is home to Somali students and traders. A wider conflict could disrupt remittances and travel routes, and Somali communities in the region may face heightened tensions or xenophobic backlash. Additionally, the involvement of Turkey, a key ally of Somalia, adds another layer of complexity, as Ankara has been a major supporter of the Somali government and its security forces.\n\nThe Somali government has not yet commented on the warning, but analysts suggest that Mogadishu will be closely monitoring the situation. Somalia has its own challenges with militant groups, and any regional instability could divert attention and resources away from its own security efforts. The international community is urging restraint, with calls for diplomatic dialogue to de-escalate tensions between Saudi Arabia and the Houthis.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/10/pakistan-oo-xuutiyiinta-uga-digtay-in-weerarrada-sacuudigu-kicin-karaan-difaac-wadajir-ah/",
